#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124238 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "266a9a4e-cb11-5e4a-a2ac-c2e734966372" :question/math-academy-id "e-5424" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, and \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068722/assets/ac61fea55da2b0f5b4c2b21b8915c7eb7fc30c576b2ca7dea830ae0aa9183dd6.png)\n\n- $A$ is not invertible\n\n- $\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^4$\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068722/assets/f57560debe62c432de44b86e2b1f569b703b9ef3faae46bcc2df18cca17a2c62.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068722/assets/65f646a200575b7299fc0278f7a5dc73cba98e293943bdb592ca24c4dd01df0f.png)\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=1 \\neq 0.\n$$\n\nSo,  according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since the matrix $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Col}(A) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{rank}(A) \\neq 4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124351 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "c1120305-c8cb-5f57-97b1-9b31dc059b4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9961" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n$\n\n- $A$ has $n$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068722/assets/7ab0bb5d64ace5e31ea02d022aa0d8696deb3b62555cfccac069ba74ff750526.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\det(A) = 0.$* $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nSince $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ must be invertible, which means $\\det(A) \\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n.$* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $A$ has $n$ pivot columns.* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=n,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $n$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124352 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "fff1daf8-5a1f-54c9-90d7-87710ca00d23" :question/math-academy-id "e-9962" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068722/assets/0ab76d251e530205b2fad22f1b4a4677370459e5cd7e19da4220c7c4baac51a3.png)\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068722/assets/c9e4725a19de7c9a07cec4b929905c3d1907871b46deb3ab197b7481aa2a0467.png)\n\nThe missing expression is $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358521 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa054469-f6b7-5561-a3c6-4ddb18025ac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358523} {:answer/id #uuid "efed5d7f-4356-5220-978e-e809c4d45513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358524} {:answer/id #uuid "365378ec-3223-5905-9912-318f8563dfc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358525} {:answer/id #uuid "d5113854-0df7-5699-972c-3780ead3afc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358526} {:answer/id #uuid "b44c5db6-1c99-5d70-82ff-9bfcf7cb44d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "365378ec-3223-5905-9912-318f8563dfc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358525} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4427eeb-5ce3-5c7e-83a1-2a01e296cb99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "622f3852-73e0-573b-9e15-ed3777600f27" :question/math-academy-id "q-141192" :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))==\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $n.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123fc04e-4909-5609-aee0-32b5f60b795e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358530} {:answer/id #uuid "947919d2-63e1-5774-b9bd-af379aeb50e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358531} {:answer/id #uuid "75e32c15-af80-5d43-b41a-6e3c9b3a99c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358532} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ef2978-6469-5b50-bb76-9cfa29f42b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358533} {:answer/id #uuid "cb69d318-2175-5a82-a86a-deca237f31d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "123fc04e-4909-5609-aee0-32b5f60b795e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358530} :answer-field/id #uuid "7082c85f-a381-57a4-a45b-30ec8ba228b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c701e87-53a1-5406-ab33-1b75f3ea593b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141199" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\n- $\\text{Null}(A)={0}$\n- $A$ is not invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69d414c6-95a7-5add-97a6-f3ec5d96aa43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358537} {:answer/id #uuid "f0809ef3-416e-522e-8055-30fe8e88c3d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358538} {:answer/id #uuid "387e38de-24b5-57ce-ba67-64345d4d80c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358539} {:answer/id #uuid "40c0961e-73c1-5b89-9609-54b61bff91b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358540} {:answer/id #uuid "0e4ac354-4f56-590a-8777-73398c8d7f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e4ac354-4f56-590a-8777-73398c8d7f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358541} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f51647-bae7-5532-b804-b3b54fdccc4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95fb61ee-6372-5926-b11d-d18f6bad7442" :question/math-academy-id "q-141200" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and  $det(A)=-1?$ \n\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $\\text{Null}(A)={0}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A)=6$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{Null}(A)={0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is invertible,  it must be $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124709 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66bf4224-09d2-5e19-b83d-a75ce4cf1a30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{dim(Col}({A}^{-1}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358543} {:answer/id #uuid "db9db227-f3d0-5805-9f88-6fa1992d6daa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Null}({A}^{-1})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358544} {:answer/id #uuid "1652c717-40eb-51b1-973a-5aecef61096a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{nullity}(A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358545} {:answer/id #uuid "204b0a17-b141-5402-aad2-734c466afa76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{dim(Col}(A))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358546} {:answer/id #uuid "794cdbef-e917-5558-a36d-cb308261765e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "204b0a17-b141-5402-aad2-734c466afa76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{dim(Col}(A))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358546} :answer-field/id #uuid "71d573b6-ab09-5dac-a4bf-fd13b6e2f207" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e871e5da-dd33-5fd3-8ed3-3fbed0583a91" :question/math-academy-id "q-141343" :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & =n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{dim(Col}(A)).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a83acbb-2700-52ee-9744-9e01aeee4198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "identity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358550} {:answer/id #uuid "2926e5db-d045-5045-bd0f-4548e32d86f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "diagonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358551} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d1f2c7-0f7f-596e-a5f7-6e4bfb62f6e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358552} {:answer/id